    "q-237493": {
      "dom_id": "step-q237493",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/4d57e8e8f607d2897a32531acd59ed0ed3f96499d9f2ff6468956be09c6cca65.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing fraction in the following division problem.\n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{5}{6}=3\\,\\frac{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $3$ wholes split into sixths.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/9e4273830a4a7c4f5cc705d6c60ae0ec2f973fd36c0bfb25bafd2024107135b7.png)\n\n- There are $5$ sixths in each group, with $3$ remaining.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/bfa0ff01c63742ffc30dcaeb2b4bd2d6c2f8c97dcfcce9e09707e54751e528aa.png)\n\nNote the following: \n\n- There are $3$ full groups.\n\n- There are $3$ sixths left over out of a possible group of $5.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n3÷\\frac{5}{6}=3\\,\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
