    "q-237485": {
      "dom_id": "step-q237485",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/f9d18c6faefcd2963ff23f948c90d71a2d0cbaa2d0dc779ff3f48a2343c86f4f.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing fraction in the following division problem.\n\n$$\n2÷\\frac{4}{7}=3\\,\\frac{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $2$ wholes split into sevenths.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/29a9dc6376b9f3ec9305da59a04e4994ab22ea8a9dd4a1c6f1101bbd41806c20.png)\n\n- There are $4$ sevenths in each group, with $2$ remaining.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/2d966707cb08c842176cc92417e3e6fc37acddb36b7261eb756a4df8bd849c1f.png)\n\nNote the following: \n\n- There are $3$ full groups.\n\n- There are $2$ sevenths left over out of a possible group of $4.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2÷\\frac{4}{7}=3\\,\\frac{2}{4}.\n$$",
