    "q-237496": {
      "dom_id": "step-q237496",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/90d15521c8aa8ef53e0659debffdb85010332cd8ba653ca52b6e36a40a05b57a.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing fraction in the following division problem.\n\n$$\n4÷\\frac{7}{8}=4\\,\\frac{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}{\\boxed{\\,\\,}}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $4$ wholes split into eighths.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/a216109fd3b33a037b060dc832e208e2d2162d5a13b405a2a06b39e5f6656668.png)\n\n- There are $7$ eighths in each group, with $4$ remaining.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/7a3a4edb355d0c6fd7138dcfe56c420a4a52faeff12d12437d4b152294e6283e.png)\n\nNote the following: \n\n- There are $4$ full groups.\n\n- There are $4$ eighths left over out of a possible group of $7.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{7}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4÷\\frac{7}{8}=4\\,\\frac{4}{7}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
