      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/62bc017c2a920b6e1014fe652ce847d76b062bf2290e2916a99b830e8e10db45.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate the value of $2÷\\frac{3}{5}.$",
      "worked_solution": "To calculate $2÷\\frac{3}{5}$, we first need to divide each of the $2$ wholes into $5$ equal parts.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/18252aca223fa7861bd1c55b53514853bb0ecb5584742ad6f6c127b71791188d.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we break this into groups of $3$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/18af0e24ce19e818f9b3ec3189db0e4edecbf06789667dbe3cfa97173ba69996.png)\n\n \n\nNote the following: \n\n- We got $\\boxed{\\text{3}}$ full groups.\n\n- There is $1$ piece left over out of a possible group of $3.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n2÷\\frac{3}{5}=\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\,\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$",
